    "sha": "14026c528d235884",
    "text": "Have you ever wondered how a refrigerator actually makes things cold? It\u2019s not magic, but it is a pretty cool trick involving a special liquid called a refrigerant. Think of this refrigerant as a tiny, busy worker that moves heat around.\n\nThe process starts inside the back of your fridge, where the refrigerant is a gas. A compressor, which is like a strong pump, squeezes this gas. When you squeeze a gas, it gets hot. This hot gas moves through coils on the back of the fridge (the part that feels warm to the touch). There, it releases its heat into the air in your kitchen. As it loses heat, the gas turns back into a liquid.\n\nNow, here\u2019s the magic part. This liquid refrigerant travels through a tiny, narrow tube called an expansion valve. When it squeezes through this tiny opening, it expands rapidly. Just like how a spray can gets cold when you press the nozzle, this expanding liquid absorbs a huge amount of heat from its surroundings. In this case, the \"surroundings\" are the inside of your fridge!\n\nSo, the refrigerant sucks the heat out of your milk, vegetables, and leftovers. It carries that heat back to the compressor, where the cycle starts all over again. The compressor pushes the heat out, the refrigerant cools down, and it goes back in to steal more heat from your food.\n\nEssentially, a refrigerator doesn\u2019t create cold; it moves heat. It acts like a heat pump, taking the warmth out of your food and dumping it into your kitchen. That\u2019s why your kitchen might feel a little warmer if the fridge is running hard. It\u2019s a continuous loop of moving heat from the cold inside to the warmer outside, keeping your snacks fresh and your ice cream solid.",
